\chapter{\texorpdfstring{The
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
Type and Error
Flow}{The  Type and Error Flow}}\label{ch22-the-resultte-type-and-error-flow}

MuDSL treats failure as an ordinary value rather than an invisible jump.
The
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
type is the toolkit's canonical carrier for a computation that can
either succeed with a value of type \texttt{T} or fail with an error of
type \texttt{E}. It is the explicit-error-flow counterpart to the
optional-value machinery of Chapter 20 and the monadic
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional} helpers of Chapter 21.
Where a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} can
express \emph{that} a computation produced nothing, a
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
can also express \emph{why}.

This chapter documents the public interface of
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
as defined in \texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp}, explains the combinators
that operate on it, and shows how to assemble fallible pipelines that
propagate the first error. The design ties directly to the first design
pillar articulated in Chapter 1: the library prefers visible,
inspectable error states over exceptions for control flow. Errors are
values, and values compose.

\section{The Type and Its Storage}\label{ch22-the-type-and-its-storage}

\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is a thin, value-semantic wrapper around a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}variant\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The success branch stores a \texttt{T} at alternative index \texttt{0};
the error branch stores an \texttt{E} at alternative index \texttt{1}.
The class holds a single private member \texttt{data\_\allowbreak{}} of
type
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}variant\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
and every query, accessor, and combinator on \texttt{Result} is a
disciplined forwarding of
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}variant} access.

Because the storage is a variant rather than a pointer or a sentinel, a
\texttt{Result} object always holds exactly one of the two payloads.
There is no ``empty'' state distinct from the error state, and there is
no additional nullable overhead beyond what
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}variant} itself requires. This
makes \texttt{Result} cheap to copy, move, and return by value, which is
essential because the type is intended to flow through chains of
combinators.

\begin{code}
namespace dsl {
template <typename T, typename E> class Result
{
  // ...
private:
  std::variant<T, E> data_;
};
}
\end{code}

The two template parameters are the success type \texttt{T} and the
error type \texttt{E}. The library does not constrain either parameter
beyond what \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}variant} requires:
both must be object types that are constructible and destructible in the
usual ways. Callers are free to choose a small scalar error such as
\texttt{int}, a descriptive
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string}, or a dedicated
error-code enumeration.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Construction: \texttt{from\_\allowbreak{}ok}
and
\texttt{from\_\allowbreak{}err}}{Construction:  and }}\label{ch22-construction-from_ok-and-from_err}

The primary, unambiguous way to build a \texttt{Result} is through the
static factories.
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}from\_\allowbreak{}ok(\allowbreak{}v)\allowbreak{}}
constructs a success result carrying \texttt{v}, while
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}from\_\allowbreak{}err(\allowbreak{}e)\allowbreak{}}
constructs an error result carrying \texttt{e}. Both factories move
their argument into the underlying variant and return a prvalue of type
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.

\begin{code}
auto good = dsl::Result<int, std::string>::from_ok (7);
auto bad  = dsl::Result<int, std::string>::from_err (std::string{"overflow"});
\end{code}

The factories are the recommended construction path because they name
the role of the argument explicitly. A reader of the call site can tell
at a glance whether the value is intended as a success or an error,
which removes the ambiguity that arises when \texttt{T} and \texttt{E}
happen to share a representation.

In addition to the factories, \texttt{Result} provides two implicit
converting constructors. The constructor
\texttt{Result(\allowbreak{}T value)\allowbreak{}} places its argument
in the success branch, and
\texttt{Result(\allowbreak{}E error)\allowbreak{}} places its argument
in the error branch. The error constructor is constrained with a
\texttt{requires (\allowbreak{}!std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}same\_\allowbreak{}as\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{} E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
clause so that, when \texttt{T} and \texttt{E} are the same type, the
compiler does not have to resolve an ambiguous overload set.

\begin{code}
dsl::Result<int, std::string> r = 42;          // success branch
dsl::Result<int, std::string> e = std::string{"io"}; // error branch
\end{code}

When \texttt{T} and \texttt{E} are distinct, both constructors
participate in overload resolution, and a value of either type converts
silently. When \texttt{T} and \texttt{E} are identical, only the success
constructor remains, and the error branch must be reached through
\texttt{from\_\allowbreak{}err} or through the private in-place
constructor that \texttt{from\_\allowbreak{}err} invokes internally.
This design keeps the common case ergonomic while preventing ambiguity
in the degenerate case.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{Ok} and \texttt{Err}
Wrappers}{The  and  Wrappers}}\label{ch22-the-ok-and-err-wrappers}

MuDSL also provides two free function templates,
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Ok} and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Err}, which package a value into
a small wrapper struct. \texttt{Ok(\allowbreak{}t)\allowbreak{}} returns
an \texttt{OkWrap\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
whose single member \texttt{value} holds \texttt{t};
\texttt{Err(\allowbreak{}e)\allowbreak{}} returns an
\texttt{ErrWrap\textless{}\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
whose single member \texttt{error} holds \texttt{e}. The wrappers deduce
their template argument from the function argument, so the caller does
not write the type.

\begin{code}
template <typename T> struct OkWrap  { T value; };
template <typename E> struct ErrWrap { E error;  };

template <typename T> OkWrap<T>  Ok  (T value);
template <typename E> ErrWrap<E> Err (E error);
\end{code}

These wrappers serve as role-marking tokens. They allow a call site to
write
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Ok(\allowbreak{}42)\allowbreak{}}
or
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Err(\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\{"bad"\})\allowbreak{}}
and let the surrounding context determine the full
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
type. They are most useful in generic code and in assignments where the
target type already fixes \texttt{T} and \texttt{E}.

\begin{code}
auto wrap = dsl::Ok (42);        // OkWrap<int>
auto erp  = dsl::Err (std::string{"oops"}); // ErrWrap<std::string>
\end{code}

For direct construction of a \texttt{Result} whose full type is already
known, the \texttt{from\_\allowbreak{}ok} and
\texttt{from\_\allowbreak{}err} factories are the clearer choice. The
wrappers and the factories cover the same conceptual ground; the
difference is whether the value or the surrounding type annotation
carries the role information.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Querying State: \texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}ok}
and
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}err}}{Querying State:  and }}\label{ch22-querying-state-is_ok-and-is_err}

Every \texttt{Result} exposes two boolean queries.
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}ok(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns
\texttt{true} when the success branch is active, and
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}err(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns
\texttt{true} when the error branch is active. The two queries are exact
complements: exactly one of them is \texttt{true} at any time, because
the underlying variant always holds exactly one alternative.

\begin{code}
bool is_ok ()  const { return std::holds_alternative<T> (data_); }
bool is_err () const { return std::holds_alternative<E> (data_); }
\end{code}

These queries are the preferred way to branch on a \texttt{Result}
without extracting a value. A caller that only needs to decide whether
to proceed can test
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}ok(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} and avoid
paying for, or risking, a value extraction. The pattern is direct and
reads like a tagged-union test, which is precisely what \texttt{Result}
is.

\begin{code}
dsl::Result<int, std::string> r = compute ();
if (r.is_ok ())
  proceed ();
else
  log (/* error access */);
\end{code}

Because the queries are \texttt{const} and trivially computed from
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}holds\_\allowbreak{}alternative},
they are suitable for use in tight loops and in conditional expressions
without performance concern. There is no allocation, no locking, and no
exception machinery involved in asking which branch is active.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Extracting Values: \texttt{unwrap} and
\texttt{unwrap\_\allowbreak{}or}}{Extracting Values:  and }}\label{ch22-extracting-values-unwrap-and-unwrap_or}

\texttt{Result} offers two value extractors.
\texttt{unwrap(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns the success value
and throws
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}runtime\_\allowbreak{}error} if
called on an error result.
\texttt{unwrap\_\allowbreak{}or(\allowbreak{}fallback)\allowbreak{}}
returns the success value if present, otherwise returns the supplied
fallback. Together they cover the two common extraction policies:
assert-and-fail, and degrade-gracefully.

\begin{code}
T unwrap () const
{
  if (!is_ok ())
    throw std::runtime_error ("dsl::Result::unwrap called on Err");
  return std::get<T> (data_);
}

T unwrap_or (T fallback) const
{
  return is_ok () ? std::get<T> (data_) : std::move (fallback);
}
\end{code}

\texttt{unwrap(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is appropriate at a point
where the program logic guarantees that the result is a success, or
where reaching the error branch is genuinely a programming error. The
thrown exception carries a fixed diagnostic string; callers that need
richer error information should inspect the error branch directly rather
than relying on \texttt{unwrap}.

\texttt{unwrap\_\allowbreak{}or(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is the
safer extractor for code that can meaningfully continue with a default.
It never throws, and it moves the fallback into the return value when
the error branch is active. Because the fallback has type \texttt{T},
the call site must commit to a concrete default, which makes the
degradation explicit and reviewable.

\begin{code}
int n = parse_int (s).unwrap_or (0);
\end{code}

Neither extractor exposes the error payload. To read the error value, a
caller tests \texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}err(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
and uses the
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}get\textless{}\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
path indirectly, through the combinators described below, or by
constructing a parallel accessor. The deliberate asymmetry keeps the
surface small: value extraction is a success-oriented operation, and
error inspection is handled through the combinators.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Transforming Success:
\texttt{map}}{Transforming Success: }}\label{ch22-transforming-success-map}

The \texttt{map} combinator applies a function to the success value,
leaving the error branch untouched. Given a callable \texttt{fn} that
accepts a \texttt{T} and returns a \texttt{U},
\texttt{map(\allowbreak{}fn)\allowbreak{}} returns a
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}U,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}:
a success result is rebuilt from
\texttt{fn(\allowbreak{}value)\allowbreak{}}, while an error result is
rebuilt from the same error. The error type \texttt{E} is preserved
across \texttt{map}.

The implementation is a faithful two-branch dispatch over the underlying
variant.

\begin{code}
template <typename Fn>
auto
map (Fn &&fn) const -> Result<decltype (fn (std::declval<T> ())), E>
{
  using U = decltype (fn (std::declval<T> ()));
  if (is_ok ())
    return Result<U, E>{ fn (std::get<T> (data_)) };
  return Result<U, E>::from_err (std::get<E> (data_));
}
\end{code}

The return type is deduced from the callable, so \texttt{map} adapts to
whatever transformation the caller supplies. A
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}int,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
mapped with
\texttt{[](\allowbreak{}int x)\allowbreak{}\{ return std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}to\_\allowbreak{}string(\allowbreak{}x)\allowbreak{}; \}}
becomes a
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The error type is carried forward unchanged, which is what makes
\texttt{map} composable along a chain of pure transformations.

\begin{code}
auto r = dsl::Result<int, std::string>::from_ok (7)
             .map ([] (int x) { return x + 1; })
             .map ([] (int x) { return std::to_string (x); });
// r : Result<std::string, std::string>
\end{code}

\texttt{map} is the right combinator when the transformation itself
cannot fail. If the transformation can fail, the caller should reach for
\texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then} instead. Using \texttt{map} with a
function that returns a \texttt{Result} produces a
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
which is almost never what is intended; the pitfall is documented below.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Transforming Error:
\texttt{map\_\allowbreak{}err}}{Transforming Error: }}\label{ch22-transforming-error-map_err}

\texttt{map\_\allowbreak{}err} is the dual of \texttt{map}: it
transforms the error payload while leaving the success value untouched.
Given a callable \texttt{fn} that accepts an \texttt{E} and returns an
\texttt{E2},
\texttt{map\_\allowbreak{}err(\allowbreak{}fn)\allowbreak{}} returns a
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E2\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
A success result is rebuilt from the same value, and an error result is
rebuilt from \texttt{fn(\allowbreak{}error)\allowbreak{}}.

\begin{code}
template <typename Fn>
auto
map_err (Fn &&fn) const -> Result<T, decltype (fn (std::declval<E> ()))>
{
  using E2 = decltype (fn (std::declval<E> ()));
  if (is_ok ())
    return Result<T, E2>::from_ok (std::get<T> (data_));
  return Result<T, E2>::from_err (fn (std::get<E> (data_)));
}
\end{code}

\texttt{map\_\allowbreak{}err} is the primary mechanism for widening or
normalizing an error type along a chain. A low-level routine that
reports a numeric code can be lifted into a higher-level routine that
reports a structured error code, without disturbing the success path.
The success type \texttt{T} is preserved, which keeps the chain's value
type stable while the error type is reshaped.

\begin{code}
enum class LowErr { overflow, underflow };
struct HighErr { LowErr code; std::string context; };

auto lift = [] (LowErr c) { return HighErr{ c, "math" }; };

auto r = dsl::Result<int, LowErr>::from_err (LowErr::overflow)
             .map_err (lift);
// r : Result<int, HighErr>
\end{code}

Because \texttt{map\_\allowbreak{}err} produces a fresh
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E2\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
even on the success branch, it can be used freely at the boundary
between two layers that disagree on the error type. The caller does not
need to branch manually; the combinator handles both cases.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Chaining Fallible Computations:
\texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then}}{Chaining Fallible Computations: }}\label{ch22-chaining-fallible-computations-and_then}

\texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then} is the flat-map combinator for
\texttt{Result}. Its callback receives the success value \texttt{T} and
returns a fresh
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}U,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}};
the combinator flattens the nesting so that the result is a
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}U,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
rather than a
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}U,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
When the input is an error, \texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then}
short-circuits: the callback is not invoked, and the error is propagated
directly.

\begin{code}
template <typename Fn>
auto
and_then (Fn &&fn) const -> decltype (fn (std::declval<T> ()))
{
  using R = decltype (fn (std::declval<T> ()));
  if (is_ok ())
    return fn (std::get<T> (data_));
  return R::from_err (std::get<E> (data_));
}
\end{code}

The short-circuit behaviour is what makes
\texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then} the building block of fallible
pipelines. Each step receives a success value only if every preceding
step succeeded, and the first error flows unchanged to the end of the
chain. The caller never writes an explicit \texttt{if} to test for
failure between steps.

\begin{code}
dsl::Result<int, std::string>
parse_int (const std::string &s);

dsl::Result<int, std::string>
validate_positive (int x)
{
  return x >= 0 ? dsl::Result<int, std::string>::from_ok (x)
                : dsl::Result<int, std::string>::from_err ("negative");
}

auto r = parse_int (s).and_then (validate_positive);
// r : Result<int, std::string>
\end{code}

The error type \texttt{E} is fixed across an
\texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then} chain. Each callback must return a
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}U,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
with the same \texttt{E} as its input, or the chain will not type-check.
When a step's natural error type differs, \texttt{map\_\allowbreak{}err}
is used at that step to widen it into the chain's common error type
before continuing.

\begin{code}
auto r = step_a (x)
             .and_then ([] (A a) {
               return step_b (a).map_err (to_chain_error);
             })
             .and_then (step_c);
\end{code}

\texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then} returns the exact type produced by its
callback, deduced through \texttt{decltype}. This means the success type
can change freely along the chain while the error type must agree. The
combinator is, in effect, the monadic bind of \texttt{Result}, and it is
the tool that turns a sequence of fallible steps into a single
expression.

\section{Recovery and Fallbacks}\label{ch22-recovery-and-fallbacks}

Not every error is fatal. MuDSL offers two complementary ways to recover
from an error result. The first is \texttt{unwrap\_\allowbreak{}or},
which collapses a
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
to a \texttt{T} by substituting a fallback when the error branch is
active. This is the simplest form of recovery: the caller abandons the
distinction between success and error and commits to a concrete value.

\begin{code}
int port = parse_port (cfg).unwrap_or (80);
\end{code}

The second form of recovery is structural rather than collapsing.
Because \texttt{Result} exposes
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}err(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} and the
\texttt{from\_\allowbreak{}ok}/\texttt{from\_\allowbreak{}err}
factories, a caller can inspect the error branch and substitute a fresh
success result, in effect rebuilding the \texttt{Result} from a recovery
value. This is the pattern to use when recovery should produce a new
success rather than a bare value, so that the result can continue to
flow through further combinators.

\begin{code}
auto recover = [] (dsl::Result<int, std::string> r)
    -> dsl::Result<int, std::string> {
  if (r.is_err ())
    return dsl::Result<int, std::string>::from_ok (0); // default
  return r;
};
\end{code}

For value-level fallbacks, \texttt{unwrap\_\allowbreak{}or} is
preferable because it is a single expression with no branching. For
pipeline-level recovery, where the result must keep its
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
shape so that downstream combinators still apply, the
inspect-and-rebuild pattern is the appropriate tool. Both patterns keep
the recovery visible at the call site rather than hiding it in an
exception handler.

\section{Building a Fallible
Pipeline}\label{ch22-building-a-fallible-pipeline}

The combinators compose into pipelines that read left to right. A
typical pipeline begins with a fallible producer, threads the value
through one or more \texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then} steps that can each
fail, applies pure transformations with \texttt{map}, and widens the
error type with \texttt{map\_\allowbreak{}err} where layers meet. The
pipeline's overall type is the success type of the last step paired with
the common error type.

\begin{code}
dsl::Result<int, std::string>
parse_count (const std::string &s)
{
  if (s.empty ())
    return dsl::Result<int, std::string>::from_err ("empty");
  // ... assume success
  return dsl::Result<int, std::string>::from_ok (std::stoi (s));
}

dsl::Result<std::vector<int>, std::string>
build_range (int count)
{
  if (count > 1024)
    return dsl::Result<std::vector<int>, std::string>::from_err ("too large");
  std::vector<int> v (count);
  std::iota (v.begin (), v.end (), 0);
  return dsl::Result<std::vector<int>, std::string>::from_ok (std::move (v));
}

auto out = parse_count (s)
               .and_then (build_range)
               .map ([] (std::vector<int> v) { return v.size (); });
// out : Result<std::size_t, std::string>
\end{code}

The pipeline reads as a sequence of intentions: parse, then build, then
measure. Each \texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then} step is a potential
failure point, and the first error short-circuits the rest. A reader
does not have to trace nested conditionals to find where the chain
stops; the structure of the expression makes the order and the failure
points explicit.

Because the error type is uniform across the chain, the final result
carries a single \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} error
regardless of which step produced it. When steps would naturally produce
different error types, a \texttt{map\_\allowbreak{}err} is inserted at
the boundary to lift each step's error into the common type. This keeps
the chain type-correct without forcing every step to share a single
error representation.

\section{Result and the Parser
Domain}\label{ch22-result-and-the-parser-domain}

\texttt{Result} is the value type that underlies several higher-level
facilities in the toolkit. The parser combinators of Chapter 23 and the
diagnostic machinery of Chapter 25 use a richer \texttt{ExpectedResult}
type whose design mirrors \texttt{Result}'s two-branch shape. The
familiarity gained here transfers directly: the same
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}ok}/\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}err} discipline
and the same \texttt{map}/\texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then} reasoning
apply, with the error payload growing to carry parser-specific
diagnostics.

A small fallible parser can be modelled directly with
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
before the full parser infrastructure is introduced. Consider a function
that parses a single integer literal and returns either an AST node or a
parse error. The signature makes the failure mode part of the contract.

\begin{code}
struct Ast { int value; };
struct ParseError { std::string message; std::size_t position; };

dsl::Result<Ast, ParseError>
parse_int_literal (std::string_view text, std::size_t pos)
{
  if (text.empty () || !std::isdigit (text.front ()))
    return dsl::Result<Ast, ParseError>::from_err ({ "not a digit", pos });
  int v = text.front () - '0';
  return dsl::Result<Ast, ParseError>::from_ok (Ast{ v });
}
\end{code}

The same routine can be lifted into a larger parse by composing it with
\texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then}. Each step that consumes input returns a
\texttt{Result} pairing the consumed value with the remaining input, and
the chain threads the remainder forward. This is, in miniature, the
structure that Chapter 23 generalizes.

\begin{code}
using Step = dsl::Result<std::pair<Ast, std::size_t>, ParseError>;

Step parse_term (std::string_view text, std::size_t pos)
{
  return parse_int_literal (text, pos)
      .and_then ([&] (Ast a) -> Step {
        return Step::from_ok ({ a, pos + 1 });
      });
}
\end{code}

The example illustrates why \texttt{Result} is the toolkit's preferred
explicit-error type: it composes without macros, without exceptions, and
without hidden control flow, and it leaves the failure reason in the
type signature for every caller to see.

\section{Result Versus Exceptions}\label{ch22-result-versus-exceptions}

Exceptions and \texttt{Result} solve the same problem with opposite
emphases. An exception propagates implicitly through the call stack
until a handler catches it; the intermediate frames need not mention the
failure mode at all. A \texttt{Result} propagates explicitly through
return values; every function that can fail says so in its signature,
and every caller must acknowledge the result at the point of call.

The explicit style has a cost: call sites are decorated with
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}ok} tests or combinator calls. It also has a
benefit: the set of values a function can return is exactly the set its
signature advertises, and there are no hidden jumps for a reader to
overlook. For a toolkit whose design pillar is visible, inspectable
error states, the trade-off is deliberate.

\texttt{Result}'s \texttt{unwrap(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is the one
place where the type crosses into exception territory. It throws
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}runtime\_\allowbreak{}error} when
called on an error result, and it should be treated as a debug assertion
rather than a recovery mechanism. Production code that needs to handle
failure should use \texttt{unwrap\_\allowbreak{}or}, the combinators, or
an explicit \texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}err} branch instead of
\texttt{unwrap}.

\section{Result Versus Maybe and
std::optional}\label{ch22-result-versus-maybe-and-stdoptional}

\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
documented in Chapter 20, and the
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional} helpers of Chapter 21
express absence without a reason. A
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
either a \texttt{T} or nothing; the caller learns that a computation
failed but not why.
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
generalizes this by attaching an \texttt{E} to the failure branch. When
the reason matters, \texttt{Result} is the right tool; when only
presence matters, \texttt{Maybe} is sufficient and lighter.

The relationship is mechanical as well as conceptual. A
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
can be reduced to a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} by
discarding the error, and a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} can
be lifted into a
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
by supplying a default error. The combinators mirror each other:
\texttt{Maybe:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map} corresponds to
\texttt{Result:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map}, and
\texttt{Maybe:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}or\_\allowbreak{}else}
corresponds to \texttt{Result}'s recovery patterns. Readers familiar
with Chapter 20 will find the combinator discipline identical in spirit.

The distinction is not merely cosmetic. A parser that returns
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}Ast\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} on
failure gives the caller nothing to report; a parser that returns
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}Ast,\allowbreak{}ParseError\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
gives the caller a message and a position. The cost of carrying the
error is one variant alternative, which is usually a worthwhile exchange
for the diagnostic value.

\section{Pitfalls and Practical
Notes}\label{ch22-pitfalls-and-practical-notes}

The most common mistake is confusing \texttt{map} with
\texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then}. \texttt{map} expects a pure function
\texttt{T -\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} U} and yields
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}U,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}};
\texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then} expects a fallible function
\texttt{T -\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}U,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and yields
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}U,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Passing a function that returns a \texttt{Result} to \texttt{map}
produces a
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}U,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
which compiles but is almost always wrong. The compiler will not catch
the intent, only the types, so the choice between the two combinators
must be made deliberately.

The second pitfall is error-type drift along a chain. An
\texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then} chain requires every step to return the
same error type \texttt{E}. When a step's natural error type differs,
the caller must insert a \texttt{map\_\allowbreak{}err} to lift it into
the chain's common type before the step is composed. Forgetting the lift
produces a type error at the \texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then} call, which
is helpful but can be cryptic when the chain is long.

A third pitfall concerns extraction. Calling
\texttt{unwrap(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} on an error result throws,
and calling \texttt{unwrap\_\allowbreak{}or} on an error result discards
the error payload. Neither accessor reveals \emph{why} the result is an
error. Code that needs the error reason must test
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}err(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} and read the
error through the combinators or through a direct branch; relying on
\texttt{unwrap} and catching the exception is not the intended pattern.

A fourth pitfall is the degenerate case \texttt{T == E}. Here the
implicit error constructor is disabled, and the error branch can only be
reached through \texttt{from\_\allowbreak{}err}. Callers that construct
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}int,\allowbreak{}int\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
results should use the factories exclusively to avoid surprising
overload resolution. The constraint is documented on the constructor and
is visible in the source.

Finally, callers should remember that \texttt{Result} is a value type,
not a pointer. Copying a \texttt{Result} copies its payload, and moving
a \texttt{Result} moves it. There is no shared state, and there is no
need to allocate. Chains of combinators produce a sequence of
temporaries that the compiler is free to elide or move, so the style is
suitable for performance-sensitive code paths.

\section{Summary}\label{ch22-summary}

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is a value-semantic sum type over
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}variant\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
that carries either a success value of type \texttt{T} or an error of
type \texttt{E}. It is constructed through the
\texttt{from\_\allowbreak{}ok} and \texttt{from\_\allowbreak{}err}
factories, through the implicit converting constructors when \texttt{T}
and \texttt{E} differ, and through the \texttt{Ok} and \texttt{Err}
role-marking wrappers. Its state is queried with
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}ok} and \texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}err}, and its
value is extracted with \texttt{unwrap} and
\texttt{unwrap\_\allowbreak{}or}.

The combinator suite provides the standard functional repertoire.
\texttt{map} transforms a success value and preserves the error;
\texttt{map\_\allowbreak{}err} transforms an error and preserves the
success; \texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then} flattens a fallible step and
short-circuits on the first error. Together they build pipelines that
propagate errors explicitly, without exceptions and without hidden
control flow. The error type must be consistent across an
\texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then} chain, and
\texttt{map\_\allowbreak{}err} is the tool for widening it at layer
boundaries.

\texttt{Result} is the explicit-error counterpart to the \texttt{Maybe}
of Chapter 20 and the \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}
helpers of Chapter 21, and it is the conceptual foundation for the
\texttt{ExpectedResult} type used by the parser combinators of Chapter
23 and the diagnostics of Chapter 25. It embodies the design pillar set
out in Chapter 1: failure is a value, and values compose.
